    <td width=3D"30%" height=3D"24"><b>Addressing Mode</b></td>
    <td width=3D"15%" height=3D"24">
      <p></p><center><b>Opcode</b></center></td>
    <td width=3D"15%" height=3D"24">
      <p></p><center><b>Bytes</b></center></td>
    <td width=3D"179" height=3D"24"><b>Cycles</b></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td width=3D"30%" height=3D"24"><a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/obeli=
sk-6502-guide/addressing.html#ABS">Absolute</a></td>
    <td width=3D"15%" height=3D"24">
      <p></p><center>$4C</center></td>
    <td width=3D"15%" height=3D"24">
      <p></p><center>3</center></td>
    <td width=3D"179" height=3D"24">3</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td width=3D"30%" height=3D"24"><a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/obeli=
sk-6502-guide/addressing.html#IND">Indirect</a>&nbsp;</td>
    <td width=3D"15%" height=3D"24">
      <p></p><center>$6C</center></td>
    <td width=3D"15%" height=3D"24">
      <p></p><center>3</center></td>
    <td width=3D"179" height=3D"24">5</td>
  </tr>
</tbody></table>

</p><p>NB:<br>
An original 6502 has does not correctly fetch the target address
if the indirect vector falls on a page boundary (e.g. $xxFF where
xx is any value from $00 to $FF). In this case fetches the LSB
from $xxFF as expected but takes the MSB from $xx00. This is fixed
in some later chips like the 65SC02 so for compatibility always
ensure the indirect vector is not at the end of the page.</p>

<h3><a name=3D"JSR"></a>JSR - Jump to Subroutine</h3>

<p>The JSR instruction pushes the address (minus one) of the return
point on to the stack and then sets the program counter to the
target memory address.</p>

<p>Processor Status after use:</p>

